{"table":"citations","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/citations/circuit-r2-26-cite-positive-mesh","record":{"id":"circuit-r2-26-cite-positive-mesh","title":"Fixed-temperature normalized mesh converges strongly","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openai/math/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-positive-temperature.tex","source_id":"circuit-r2-26-source-positive","locator":"07-positive-temperature.tex:452–532; lem:eams-positive-continuum, eq:euler-normalization-bound, eq:martingale-euler-bound, eq:value-riemann-bound"},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/citations/circuit-r2-26-cite-positive-mesh","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["associated_record"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/alternative","anchor":"alternative","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"citation_ids"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/alternative-assembly","anchor":"alternative-assembly","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"citation_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/alternative","anchor":"alternative","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"citation_ids","text":"\n\n<a id=\"positive-temperature-route\"></a>\n\n### 11.1 The separate positive-temperature route\n\nThe positive-temperature appendix supplies a separate classical input to\nthe same synthesis and seed-removal theorems. Fix $\\beta_0>1$, let\n$\\mu_{\\beta_0}$ be its Parisi measure, and write\n$q=q_{\\beta_0}=\\max\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_{\\beta_0}$.\nHere $\\gamma(t)=\\beta_0\\mu_{\\beta_0}([0,t])$,\n$m=\\phi_{\\beta_0,x}$, $a=\\phi_{\\beta_0,xx}$ and $X$ belong to the\npositive-temperature PDE, whose terminal value is\n$\\beta_0^{-1}\\log(2\\cosh(\\beta_0x))$. They are distinct from the\nzero-temperature objects in §§1–8. The input credited to\n[Lopatto, arXiv:2607.11756v3, Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.11756v3)\ngives $\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_{\\beta_0}=[0,q]$ for $\\beta_0>1$;\nits smooth density below $q$ gives the regularity used on a fixed\n$[0,T]$ with $0<T<q$.\n\nThe normalization can be checked directly against Lopatto's PDE.\nWriting $\\widehat\\Phi_{\\beta_0}(t,y)$ for his solution with terminal\nvalue $\\log\\cosh y$ and covariance factor $\\beta_0^2$, set\n$$\n\\phi_{\\beta_0}(t,x)\n =\\frac{\\widehat\\Phi_{\\beta_0}(t,\\beta_0x)+\\log2}{\\beta_0},\n\\quad m=\\widehat\\Phi_y(t,\\beta_0x),\\quad\n a=\\beta_0\\widehat\\Phi_{yy}(t,\\beta_0x).\n$$\nHis diffusion $\\widehat X$ has noise coefficient $\\beta_0$; hence\n$X=\\widehat X/\\beta_0$ has unit noise and drift\n$\\beta_0\\mu_{\\beta_0}([0,t])m$. His identity\n$\\beta_0^2\\mathbb E\\widehat\\Phi_{yy}(t,\\widehat X_t)^2=1$\nis exactly $\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$. This is a fixed-temperature\nchange of variables, not convergence of either Parisi measure as\n$\\beta_0\\to\\infty$. [Lopatto, arXiv:2607.11756v3, equations\n(1.3), (2.1)–(2.2) and Lemma 2.2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.11756v3).\n\nThe source derives $\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$ on $[0,q]$ and\n$1-q\\le\\beta_0^{-1}$. The value estimate is\n$$\n\\int_0^T\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)\\,dt\n\\ge P_*-\\frac{1/4+\\log2}{\\beta_0}-(q-T).\n$$\nTo recover its constants, martingale contact first gives\n$\\int_0^t\\mathbb E a(s,X_s)^2ds=t$ on $[0,q]$; continuity yields\nunit second moment including the endpoints. On $[q,1]$, $\\gamma=\\beta_0$,\nso Cole–Hopf gives\n$$\n\\phi(q,x)=\\beta_0^{-1}\\log(2\\cosh\\beta_0x)+\\beta_0(1-q)/2,\n\\quad m(q,x)=\\tanh(\\beta_0x),\\quad a(q,x)=\\beta_0\\operatorname{sech}^2(\\beta_0x).\n$$\nThe endpoint identity $\\mathbb E a(q,X_q)^2=1$ is also needed.\nCauchy–Schwarz gives\n$$\n1-q=\\mathbb E\\operatorname{sech}^2(\\beta_0X_q)\n\\le\\bigl(\\mathbb E\\operatorname{sech}^4(\\beta_0X_q)\\bigr)^{1/2}\n=\\beta_0^{-1}.\n$$\nThis is the endpoint argument in [OpenAI (2026b), Appendix A,\nLemma “A positive-temperature value bound”][q-positive].\nItô's product rule and the value evolution give\n$$\n\\int_0^q\\mathbb E a=\\mathbb E[X_qm(q,X_q)]-\\int_0^qt\\gamma(t)dt,\n\\quad\n\\mathbb E\\phi(q,X_q)=\\phi(0,0)+\\tfrac12\\int_0^qt\\gamma(t)dt.\n$$\nTo compare this integral with the spin optimum, define the expected\nfree energy in energy units by\n$$\nF_{n,\\beta_0}=(n\\beta_0)^{-1}\\mathbb E\\log\\sum_\\sigma\ne^{\\beta_0H_n(\\sigma)},\\qquad\nF_{\\beta_0}=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}F_{n,\\beta_0}.\n$$\nThe fixed-temperature limit is supplied by the positive-temperature\nParisi formula cited below. The finite-size soft-maximum inequality\ngives $F_{\\beta_0}\\ge P_*$. Using\n$x\\tanh(\\beta_0x)\\ge\\beta_0^{-1}\\log(2\\cosh\\beta_0x)\n-\\beta_0^{-1}\\log2$ and retaining the penalty on $[q,1]$ yields\n$$\n\\int_0^q\\mathbb E a\\ge F_{\\beta_0}\n-\\frac{\\beta_0(1-q)^2}{4}-\\frac{\\log2}{\\beta_0}.\n$$\nThe coefficient $1/4$ comes from the two tail contributions, since\n$\\gamma=\\beta_0$ above $q$:\n$$\n\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}\\int_q^1 t\\,dt\n -\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}(1-q)\n=-\\frac{\\beta_0}{4}(1-q)^2.\n$$\nThe cutoff removes at most $q-T$, because $0\\le\\mathbb E a\\le1$.\nThis proves the displayed estimate. Choose first a sufficiently large\nfinite $\\beta_0$, then $T<q$ close enough to $q$. This route obtains the existence and value of the\nground-state limit from the positive-temperature Parisi formula of\n[Talagrand (2006), Theorem 1.1, pp. 222–223](https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2006.163.221).\nAt each fixed $\\beta_0$, his covariance hypotheses (1.3)–(1.5) hold with\n$\\xi(t)=\\beta_0^2t^2/2$ and error $c(n)=\\beta_0^2/(2n)$.\nHis partition sum (1.14) is unnormalized; dividing its logarithmic\nfree energy by $\\beta_0$ gives the convention here, including the\n$\\log2/\\beta_0$ entropy term. The finite-size inequality used above is\n$$\nF_{n,\\beta_0}-(\\log2)/\\beta_0\\le n^{-1}\\mathbb E\\max H_n\\le F_{n,\\beta_0}.\n$$\nIt does not invoke the zero-temperature companion\nto justify that step. Write $M_n=n^{-1}\\mathbb E\\max H_n$. The same\nuniform entropy bound gives\n$$\n|F_{n,\\beta_0}-P_*|\\le |M_n-P_*|+\\frac{\\log2}{\\beta_0}.\n$$\nThus arbitrary joint $n,\\beta_0\\to\\infty$ are permitted for these scalar\nfree-energy values. This says nothing about joint convergence of measures,\nPDE derivatives, message calculations or circuit words.\n[OpenAI (2026b), Appendix A][q-positive].\n\nThe remaining limits have distinct tasks. At fixed\n$\\beta_0,T,\\delta,L$, with $\\delta>0$, integer $L\\ge1$ and $L\\delta\\le T$, the degree limit $D\\to\\infty$ identifies the\nfinite message law and its edge value. Let $U_0,U_1,\\ldots$ be independent\nstandard normals. Put\n$$\nX_0^\\delta=\\sqrt\\delta\\,U_0,\\qquad\nX_{r+1}^\\delta=X_r^\\delta+\\delta\\gamma(r\\delta)m(r\\delta,X_r^\\delta)\n                   +\\sqrt\\delta\\,U_{r+1},\\qquad\nc_{\\delta,r}=(\\mathbb E a(r\\delta,X_r^\\delta)^2)^{-1/2}.\n$$\nThe finite-degree output is a message calculation on the $(D+1)$-regular\ntree, where $\\partial i$ is the set of neighbours of vertex $i$.\nWrite $t_r=r\\delta$ and $b(t,x)=\\gamma(t)m(t,x)$.\nInitialise independent standard normal labels $g_i$, with\n$u^0_{i\\to j}=u_i^0=g_i$, $A^{-1}_{i\\to j}=1$ and\n$x^0_{i\\to j}=x_i^0=\\sqrt\\delta\\,g_i$. For $0\\le r<L$ set\n$$\nu^{r+1}_{i\\to j}=D^{-1/2}\\!\\sum_{\\substack{v\\in\\partial i\\\\v\\ne j}}\n A^{r-1}_{v\\to i}u^r_{v\\to i},\\qquad\nu^{r+1}_i=(D+1)^{-1/2}\\!\\sum_{v\\in\\partial i}\n A^{r-1}_{v\\to i}u^r_{v\\to i}.\n$$\nEach $x$ then takes the Euler step\n$x^{r+1}=x^r+\\delta b(t_r,x^r)+\\sqrt\\delta\\,u^{r+1}$ with its corresponding\ndirected-edge or vertex message. The directed-edge law fixes the common\nnormaliser:\n$$\nc_{D,r}=(\\mathbb E a(t_r,x^r_{i\\to j})^2)^{-1/2},\\quad\nA^r_{i\\to j}=c_{D,r}a(t_r,x^r_{i\\to j}),\\quad\nB_i^r=c_{D,r}a(t_r,x_i^r),\\quad\nz_i^L=\\sqrt\\delta\\sum_{r=1}^{L}B_i^{r-1}u_i^r.\n$$\nThe one-step lag in $A^{r-1}u^r$ gives conditional centring from the new\nouter Gaussian layer; successive finite-degree messages need not be\nindependent. At fixed $\\delta,L$, the joint vertex messages converge to\nthe independent $U_r$ above, and $z_i^L$ converges to\n$\\sqrt\\delta\\sum_{r=1}^Lc_{\\delta,r-1}a(t_{r-1},X^\\delta_{r-1})U_r$.\nThe source's separate edge calculation gives\n$$\n\\lim_{D\\to\\infty}\\frac{D+1}{2\\sqrt D}\\mathbb E[z_o^Lz_v^L]\n  =\\delta\\sum_{r=1}^{L-1}c_{\\delta,r}\\mathbb E a(r\\delta,X_r^\\delta),\n\\qquad o\\sim v.\n$$\nOnly afterwards take $\\delta\\downarrow0$ with $L=\\lfloor T/\\delta\\rfloor$.\nThe source bounds the discrepancy from\n$\\int_0^T\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)\\,dt$ by $C(\\beta_0,T)\\sqrt\\delta$.\nA separate norm-convergence argument with a uniform first occupation\nmoment identifies this edge limit with the rooted energy. A\nfinite-dimensional central limit theorem alone does not identify that\nobservable. [OpenAI (2026b), Appendix A][q-positive].\n\n","span":{"start_byte":199557,"end_byte":206760,"start_line":4305,"end_line":4469},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=alternative","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=alternative"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/alternative-assembly","anchor":"alternative-assembly","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"citation_ids","text":"\nAfter clipping and bounded approximation, the result enters the same\nsynthesis and seed-removal route as the primary construction. Its source\ndependencies and fixed-temperature choice of parameters remain separate\nfrom the retained zero-temperature formal interfaces.\n[OpenAI (2026b), Appendix A][q-positive].\n\n<a id=\"temperature-route-comparison\"></a>\n\n### 11.1.1 Where the temperature routes meet\n\nBoth routes produce a bounded finite classical calculation whose rooted\nenergy is close to $P_*$. They reach that common input by different\ntheorems and different finite approximations.\n\n**Variational input and support.** The zero-temperature route fixes an\nattained minimizer $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$ with terminal value $|x|$ and\nuses full relative Stieltjes support on $[0,1)$. The positive-temperature\nroute fixes a finite $\\beta_0>1$ and its Parisi probability measure, with\nterminal value $\\beta_0^{-1}\\log(2\\cosh\\beta_0x)$; it uses Lopatto's\ninterval-support result on $[0,q_{\\beta_0}]$.\n\n**Scalar error.** Before discretization, the zero-temperature loss is at\nmost $1-T$ for $T<1$. The positive-temperature loss is at most\n$$\n(1/4+\\log2)/\\beta_0+(q_{\\beta_0}-T),\\qquad T<q_{\\beta_0}.\n$$\n\n**Finite energy.** In the zero-temperature route, a first-chaos projection\nidentifies the explicit Gaussian coefficient sum with\n$\\operatorname{Re}\\langle F_N,\\mathsf L F_N\\rangle$. The alternative\nroute first calculates finite-degree messages and edge energy, then uses\na rooted norm and occupation argument.\n\n**Order of approximation.** At zero temperature, fix $T$ before refining\nthe scalar mesh, and choose a finite mesh before regularization and synthesis.\nAt positive temperature, fix $\\beta_0,T,\\delta,L$ for the message degree\nlimit, then refine $\\delta$ at fixed $\\beta_0,T$.\n\nBoth routes then use the same bounded approximation, exclusions, seeded\nsynthesis, seed removal and fixed-word transfer. The quantum construction\nis shared; its classical input has been supplied independently.\n\nHere the displayed positive-temperature estimate is exactly the one\nderived above, rather than an assertion of convergence of zero-temperature\nsupports. One can choose $\\beta_0$ and $T$ finitely for the required\nerror and then stop. The later circuit argument needs that finite\nchoice; it never asks for a joint limit of the temperature, message\nmesh, graph degree and circuit depth. [OpenAI (2026b), Appendix A,\nProposition “The independent positive-temperature construction”][q-positive];\n[§6][q-assembly].\n","span":{"start_byte":216790,"end_byte":219297,"start_line":4687,"end_line":4737},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=alternative-assembly","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=alternative-assembly"}],"lenses":{},"relations":[{"family":"provenance","id":"field:0811ac5c920571b7ec1e7130bd025e8237fd320460aee2103437a7210ebfeebf","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/citations/circuit-r2-26-cite-positive-mesh","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/sources/circuit-r2-26-source-positive","kind":"source_id","semantic_class":"provenance_field_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/citations/circuit-r2-26-cite-positive-mesh","source_field":"source_id"},{"family":"provenance","id":"field:a1f588052b4face63c39b95a0e3552e332c13accbdbc33e4e943efd312ace1b9","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.positive_mesh","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/citations/circuit-r2-26-cite-positive-mesh","kind":"citation_ids","semantic_class":"provenance_field_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/circuit.positive_mesh","source_field":"citation_ids"}],"neighborhood_href":"/api/v1/neighborhood?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&ref=sha256%3A9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5%2Fcitations%2Fcircuit-r2-26-cite-positive-mesh"}